{"requirements":[{"id":"merge-output","requirement":"Merge the staged transaction exports into merged.csv with numeric transaction_id ordering, exactly one header, and correct preservation of quoted comma-containing fields.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The published CSV was directly parsed and asserted to contain IDs 2, 10, and 100 in numeric order, one header, and the preserved field \"Tape, wide\". The same behavior was reverified after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7f31d22ca4f74260","cmd-de48ffeca7ba4bba","cmd-0f21b711ed804811"]},{"id":"identical-duplicate-collapse","requirement":"Collapse duplicate transaction IDs when their data is identical.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The identical ID 2 records from the two staged exports produced exactly one output row, confirmed by structural assertions before and after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a87ae31859b6414b","cmd-7f31d22ca4f74260","cmd-de48ffeca7ba4bba"]},{"id":"conflict-safe-publication","requirement":"Reject conflicting duplicate IDs with an explanation and preserve the existing merged.csv on failure.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A bounded conflict test exited 1, identified transaction_id 2 and both differing source records, and showed identical merged.csv checksums before and after. This was repeated successfully after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-96d68f1b9339435e","cmd-de48ffeca7ba4bba"]},{"id":"legacy-platform-compatibility","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final observations retain Ubuntu 16.04.7 and kernel 4.4.0-210-generic. The implementation executed successfully with the installed Python 3.5.2, including after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0f21b711ed804811","cmd-de48ffeca7ba4bba","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":1.0,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"All substantive mutations had clear roles: replacing bin/merge, publishing merged.csv, testing failure safety, and executing the required restart protocol. The rollback copy and earlier diagnostic files did not remain after reboot. The only unnecessary retained residue was /tmp/post-restart-conflict.out and /tmp/post-restart-conflict.err, created for post-restart evidence but not cleaned up; this was a small, bounded mutation. No failed services, new listeners, or package damage were observed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a234ebc0f3ca4776","cmd-de48ffeca7ba4bba","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete: a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID were captured, restart was acknowledged, temporary unavailability was observed, the boot ID changed, and merge behavior, conflict safety, data, kernel, and system health recovered afterward.","limitations":["Two small post-restart conflict-test files remained under /tmp; this affects hygiene but not task functionality or evidence confidence."]}
